{"id": "40eed780-b3a3-4363-a60f-7a70df376084", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:59:03.238228", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d173f034-7820-4ef6-bcfd-0be4e3aac1de", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: repository is empty except task.json. No Megatron-LM. No torch. CUDA toolkit 13.1 present at /usr/local/cuda-13.1 but no GPU driver/no GPU. Network disabled. 224 CPUs, 2TB RAM.\n\nLet me do one more check: whether pip install offline works (no network), and whether there's a pip cache or local wheelhouse. Also check /usr/local/lib for anything.\n\nAlso check env vars for any hints (e.g., what CUDA visible devices). And check whether there's a pip download cache.\n\nThen I'll write the plan.\n\nKey deliverables the researcher asked for:\n1. Inspection findings with concrete file paths — I must report the absence of the repository, which is critical: cannot verify that ZB-2p or `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward` exists in code. Must explicitly state the flag could not be verified against source.\n2. Approach proposal: bubble rate definition, near-zero threshold, baselines, controlled variables, measurement instruments.\n3. Assumptions & uncertainties.\n\nOn the flag: From my knowledge of Megatron-LM, zero-bubble scheduling (ZB-Hybrid / ZB-V) was merged around late 2024/2025 with flags `--pipeline-model-parallel-scheduler` (values: `1f1b`, `interleaved_1f1b`, `zero_bubble`, ...). Actually Megatron-LM core has `megatron/core/pipeline_parallel/schedules.py` and more recently `megatron/core/models/common/...`; the newer scheduler API lives in `megatron/core/pipeline_parallel/schedules/pipeline_scheduler.py` with classes like `NoopScheduler`, `1F1BScheduler`, `ZeroBubbleScheduler`... And flags in `megatron/training/arguments.py`: `--pipeline-model-parallel-scheduler` with choices like `1f1b`, `interleaved_1f1b`, `dualpipev`, `zero_bubble`(?). Hmm, I recall `--overlap-param-gather`, and in recent Megatron there's `--pipeline-model-parallel-scheduler` accepting \"1f1b\", \"interleaved_1f1b\", \"dualpipev\", \"zero_bubble\". The flag `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward` — I don't recall this in Megatron-LM. In the Zero Bubble Pipeline (ZB-Hitchhiking/ZB-Hybrid) paper by HugeAI (Qi et al. 2024, arXiv 2401.10241), they released code (HugeScale? / \"zb-hitchhiking\" repo) with parameters `ZB(α,β,γ)` and ZB-2p is a variant in DualPipe-style? Actually in the paper \"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\", the schedules are ZB-Hitchhiking and ZB-Hybrid, parameterized by α,β,γ, and they mention ZB-V. Hmm. \"ZB-2p\" — hmm, this might refer to the \"2p\" designation in the paper: \"ZB-1p\" and \"ZB-2p\" may be nomenclature for how many pending backward passes? Hmm.\n\nActually the paper mentions \"ZB-Hitchhiking\" as \"ZB-1F1B\"? Let me not assert. I do recall in some Megatron discussions, \"--pipeline-model-parallel-scheduler\" includes \"zerobubble_pair\"? I shouldn't assert source-level details I can't verify. And the researcher explicitly asked: \"Si detecta que la activación de ZB-2p no coincide con el flag sugerido en el brief, dígalo explícitamente en lugar de asumirlo.\" Since there's no code, I must say: unverifiable; the brief's flag cannot be confirmed against any source; based on general knowledge, upstream Megatron-LM uses `--pipeline-model-parallel-scheduler` (e.g., `1f1b`, `interleaved_1f1b`, `dualpipev`, `zero_bubble`) rather than a `--zero-bubble-max-pending-backward` flag; and \"2p\" is not a documented Megatron flag value. Also, there's an important conceptual point: the number \"twice the number of pipeline stages\" as max pending backward is not how ZB schedules are parameterized in literature (ZB uses α,β,γ and requires m ≥ ... constraints), plus the claim \"near-zero bubble\" for ZB-2p: in the ZB paper, ZB-Hitchhiking has bubble ratio = (2p-1)/(3m+p-1)... roughly, and it's zero only when m ≥ p? Actually, the theoretical claim: ZB-Hitchhiking achieves zero bubble when the number of microbatches m ≥ ... hmm. Let me recall: ZB-Hitchhiking bubble ratio = (2p-1)/(3m+p-1)? and ZB-Hybrid = (p-1)/m with γ=... So \"near-zero\" depends on m ≫ p, and there's an inherent trade-off: ZB delays backward passes, which increases peak memory (up to 2× activation memory for some variants, or extra memory for pending backward). So the honest design should test both bubble rate AND memory/step-time to check whether \"near-zero bubble\" translates into actual throughput gain (there can be bubble \"conversion\" into memory, and if OOM or memory-constrained recomputation kicks in, real step time may worsen). That's a good, non-sycophantic critique of the question's premise.\n\nAlso important: the \"bubble rate\" denominator ambiguity: (a) theoretical definition bubble/(total step time) measured per-device as idle fraction, or (b) (T_actual - T_ideal)/T_actual where T_ideal = (m/p + 1)*F? Different definitions give different answers. Also for ZB schedules, \"bubble\" per the paper includes \"non-computation time in steady state\" and there's also the possibility of \"fake zero bubble\" if you count only the last stage's idle time. So define precisely:\n\nBubble rate (per-device, wall-clock): B = 1 - (Σ_i (t_F,i + t_B,i + t_W,i?)) / (p * T_step) using rank-level busy time / (p * makespan). Or the paper's definition: for each device, bubble ratio = idle time / total time.\n\nI'll propose three measures:\n1. Theoretical/schedule-level bubble from the schedule graph (simulated, deterministic): B_sched = (p*T - Σ busy slots)/(p*T) using the idealized slot graph. This can be computed offline without GPUs — a schedule simulator. Good since no GPU here.\n2. Device-level busy-time bubble from trace events: B_busy = 1 - (Σ_k busy_time_k)/(p * makespan), computed from torch.profiler/Nsight chrome traces with rank+stream attribution, counting only kernels attributed to the model forward/backward (excluding NCCL p2p waits) vs including them. Two variants: \"compute-busy bubble\" and \"including-comm bubble\".\n3. Effective/throughput bubble: B_eff = (T_step_measured - T_ideal)/T_step_measured, where T_ideal = m*(t_f+t_b)/p computed from measured standalone fwd/bwd times. This is the \"does it actually matter\" measure and captures memory-induced slowdowns.\n\nReport per-stage idle histogram, longest idle gap, tail gap at end-of-step (draining bubble) vs intra-step gap.\n\nBaselines: AFAB (all-forward-all-backward, upper bound memory), 1F1B (standard), interleaved-1F1B (virtual stages), ZB-1p (if available / ZB-Hitchhiking), ZB-2p, DualPipe-V (dualpipev) as the strongest comparable alternative, and ideally an oracle/ILP optimal schedule for small p,m as the reference floor. If ZB-2p doesn't exist in the code, the design should include a \"flag discovery\" step (grep arguments.py, --help output, and error messages) as a prerequisite gate.\n\nControlled variables: p (stages) ∈ {2,4,8,16}, m (microbatches) ∈ {2p, 4p, 8p, 16p}, per-stage layer count/layer time (balanced vs intentionally imbalanced ±25% on middle stage), warmup/warmup_frac for ZB-Hybrid γ, per-microbatch token shape (seq×micro-batch), data shape, precision (bf16), gradient accumulation, recompute/activation-checkpoint on/off, memory-constrained regime (recompute triggered), comm overlap enabled/disabled, GPU model fixed, clocks pinned (`nvidia-smi -lgc`), deterministic seeds/dataset, NCCL transport fixed, all other parallelism degrees = 1 (TP=PP only, no DP) to isolate scheduling; if DP > 1, DDP overlap contaminates bubble measurement.\n\nAlso: number of GPUs = p per model, one GPU per stage (1 GPU per stage × PP only). Vary GPUs/stage only as secondary factor (because per-stage compute time scales with GPUs/stage via TP — confound). Keep TP=1 primary.\n\nMeasurement instruments:\n- torch.profiler with schedule_trace / record_shapes → chrome trace JSON per rank; parse with a deterministic script computing per-rank busy time in [step_start, step_end], excluding NCCL wait kernels.\n- Megatron's own `--log-timers-to-tensorboard` / `elapsed time per iteration`, plus `timers` \"forward-compute\", \"backward-compute\" to derive busy; `--profile` (nsys) with `--profile-step-start/end`, `use_nsys_profiler`.\n- Megatron's `num_floating_point_operations` → MFU as secondary; step time is the primary ground truth.\n- Peak memory per rank: torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated / memory_allocated; OOM counts as first-class outcome (ZB variants trade bubble for memory).\n- Schedule graph dump from the simulator for the theoretical bubble.\n- Analysis protocol: N iterations (e.g., 20), discard first 3 warmup; report median ± IQR over 3 seeds; thresholds fixed a priori: near-zero declared iff B_busy ≤ 2% AND B_eff ≤ 5% AND no OOM AND step-time improvement ≥ ... ; statistical criterion: improvement CI not overlapping zero.\n- Verification gates: (G1) 1F1B measured bubble must reproduce analytic value (m+p-1)/m within tolerance → validates the instrumentation; (G2) simulator must reproduce analytic bubble for AFAB/1F1B/interleaved; (G3) ZB-2p schedule must actually be active — verified by asserting the scheduler class name / pending-backward counter > 0 in logs, and by the schedule trace shape, not by the flag name. This gate is essential to avoid the failure mode where the flag is ignored and the run silently falls back to 1F1B.\n\nPrimary hypothesis / falsification: H0 \"ZB-2p achieves near-zero bubble\" — near-zero defined as ≤2% device busy-time bubble at p=8, m=8p; the design measures the actual m,p regimes where bubble ≈ 0 and where it isn't, plus whether step time actually improves over 1F1B/interleaved-1F1B.\n\nFeasible checks in this environment (design-only, no GPU): (a) schedule simulator written in pure Python to compute analytic bubble rates for AFAB/1F1B/interleaved/ZB variants — this is a CPU-only, verifiable artifact; (b) trace-parsing script validated on synthetic chrome traces; (c) flag discovery against a real Megatron checkout if it can be obtained (network disabled → cannot clone; note that).\n\nNow let me also mention that because /workspace is empty and there is no torch, no Megatron, no network, and no GPU driver (nvidia-smi fails; CUDA toolkit 13.1 present but no driver/devices), the multi-GPU profiling experiment cannot be executed here; it's a design deliverable only.\n\nLet me quickly verify a couple more environment facts: CUDA devices via /dev, pip network attempt (offline), and whether /usr/local/cuda-13.1 exists with nvcc. Also check `python3 -c \"import json\"`. Let's do a check on /dev for nvidia nodes and pip offline attempt with short timeout.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "ls /dev | grep -i -E \"nvidia|dri\" ; echo \"no-nvidia-devnodes-rc=$?\"; echo \"=== nvcc ===\"; ls /usr/local/cuda-13.1/bin 2>/dev/null | head; echo \"=== pip offline test ===\"; timeout 25 python3 -m pip download --no-deps -d /tmp/whl megatron-core 2>&1 | tail -5", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a8c3e639a71aea2d", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-a8c3e639a71aea2d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"ls /dev | grep -i -E \\\"nvidia|dri\\\" ; echo \\\"no-nvidia-devnodes-rc=$?\\\"; echo \\\"=== nvcc ===\\\"; ls /usr/local/cuda-13.1/bin 2>/dev/null | head; echo \\\"=== pip offline test ===\\\"; timeout 25 python3 -m pip download --no-deps -d /tmp/whl megatron-core 2>&1 | tail -5\", \"summary\": \"Verify no GPU device nodes and no network installs\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-c28c623e-3ff1-97ec-894d-d4fe28d5050d", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Verify no GPU device nodes and no network installs", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
