{"id": "da397bed-d504-4a7b-9c65-20acb7f70a90", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:08:24.864655", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "eb68cbb2-095c-4b5b-9d74-42915467d0dc", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Aprobado. Antes de redactar el diseño completo, valido que el instrumento de diseño (simulador CPU-only + descomposición de bubble) es construible y que las fórmulas analíticas que uso como control G1 se reproducen — esto es *validación de instrumento*, no el experimento."}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved and wants the full design with named sections. I should produce a comprehensive design document. I may also want to do a small feasibility check: maybe build a quick simulator prototype? The researcher said no execution of experiments; building the simulator is an artifact, but they said present it as design/validation artifact. I could optionally validate the analytic formulas for 1F1B/AFAB/interleaved with a small simulator to make the design concrete and to check G1 feasibility — that's a CPU-only instrument validation, not the experiment. That would strengthen the design (evidence that the simulator approach works). But budget: actor_step_budget 60. I have used ~8. It's allowed and adds value: showing G1 works (simulator reproduces (p-1)/(m+p-1) and general b/f formula). Let me do a compact simulation to derive and verify the general bubble formula for 1F1B with b ≠ f.\n\nLet me derive the general 1F1B makespan: p stages, m microbatches, forward f, backward b per microbatch per stage. 1F1B: warmup pp = p - s microbatches for stage s... Standard: stage s (0-indexed) does warmup of (p-1-s) forwards, then steady state, then cooldown. Makespan formula for 1F1B with f,b:\n\nKnown: for stage s, number of warmup forwards w_s = p - 1 - s. Time for stage s ≈ ... A cleaner approach: simulate. Let me write a quick list-scheduling simulator for 1F1B and compare against candidate analytic formula.\n\nCandidate general formula (from literature, e.g. \"optimizing pipelines...\", also ZB paper's bubble ratio definitions): bubble ratio for 1F1B = (p-1)·(f+b) / (m·(f+b) + (p-1)·(f+b)) = (p-1)/(m+p-1) when... hmm that's the f=b-ish formula. Actually with general f,b: the 1F1B makespan = (m + p - 1)·f + m·b + something? Let's think about the last stage: stage p-1 does no warmup; it does f,b,f,b,... for all m microbatches but must wait for activations from upstream. Its start is delayed by (p-1)·f (forward pipeline fill). Then it does m·(f+b) work, but it also has to wait for upstream backward gradients? In 1F1B, stage p-1's backward for microbatch i happens after its forward; gradient flows backward: stage s's backward for microbatch i can only start after stage s+1's backward for microbatch i completes. So the drain of the backward pass: last stage starts backward of microbatch 0 at time (p-1)f + f + b... hmm, actually backward of the last stage can start after its own forward completes. Then stage p-2's backward of mb0 needs the gradient from stage p-1 → delayed. So makespan ≈ (p-1)·f [forward fill] + ... plus a drain at end where stage 0's last backward must wait for stage p-1's last backward: the last backward of stage 0 finishes at T. Roughly:\n\nT_1F1B = m·(f+b) + (p-1)·f + (p-1)·b·? Let me just simulate with a simple event-driven scheduler to check the formula empirically. Then bubble = 1 - m(f+b)/(p·T).\n\nLet me do it: quick Python simulator with dependency constraints, greedy as-soon-as-possible respecting 1F1B memory constraint (stage s holds at most p-1-s pending activations). Simplify: schedule 1F1B explicitly per stage as: warmup forwards (w_s = p-1-s), then alternate F,B for the remaining m - w_s microbatches, then cooldown backwards w_s. With global dependency constraints (F_{i,s} after F_{i,s-1}, B_{i,s} after B_{i,s+1} and after F_{i,s}). A greedy ASAP schedule respecting the per-stage program order should give the makespan. I can implement this easily with a small loop: iterate until all tasks done; for each stage, its next task in program order, schedule when all deps are ready. This is a standard list scheduling and it converges.\n\nLet me write it, and also AFAB and interleaved-1F1B, and compare against formulas. This gives G1 evidence and the general formula for b/f ≠ 1. Also implement ZB(α,β,γ)-style scheduler as a general simulator (the design's simulator artifact) — that would be nice to show feasibility, but careful not to over-claim correctness of ZB. Maybe implement a generic ASAP scheduler with a \"policy\" that determines the task order per stage, including a ZB-Hitchhiking-like policy. Hmm, that's risky to get right and I shouldn't present unverified ZB results. But for a design deliverable, showing that a generic ASAP simulator framework runs and reproduces 1F1B analytic bubble is enough.\n\nLet me do a moderate implementation: generic ASAP + policies AFAB, 1F1B, interleaved v=2. Check formulas. Also check that with b=2f, 1F1B bubble ratio equals what formula.\n\nI'll keep it under a few minutes.\n\nThen deliver the full design as `answer` in end_researcher_turn stage=result, with all named sections (a)-(f), plus the two budget sizes, decision rule, P0 branching procedure, etc.\n\nLet me write the simulator.\n\nDesign of simulator:\n- tasks: F[i][s], B[i][s], W[s] (weight update / optimizer per stage, optional).\n- deps: F[i][s] after F[i][s-1] (s>0); B[i][s] after B[i][s+1] (s<p-1); B[i][s] after F[i][s]; F[i+1][s] after F[i][s] if no other microbatch in flight? Not needed generally, but per-stage resource: each stage processes one task at a time → program order per stage (permutation).\n- policy = ordering of microbatch indices for F and B at each stage. For 1F1B stage s: F order = 0..m-1, B order = 0..m-1, with the constraint that stage s does at most w_s = p-1-s outstanding forwards before first backward. Implement by per-stage ready queue with memory constraint: stage s can issue F[i] only if (#F issued - #B completed) <= w_s... Actually the 1F1B memory constraint: stage s holds at most (p-1-s)+1 activations? Standard: number of warmup microbatches = p-1-s. So allowed outstanding = p-1-s. Hmm, for the last stage (s=p-1) warmup = 0, meaning it does F then immediately B. Right.\n\nWait: in Megatron, num_warmup_microbatches = min(m, p - rank - 1). Yes.\n\nImplement generic: for each stage, we have a queue of pending F tasks (in order) and pending B tasks (in order). At each step, choose the next eligible task according to policy: for 1F1B, alternate: if outstanding < w_limit and F pending → F; else if B pending → B; else F. For AFAB: all F first then all B (outstanding limit = m). For interleaved v: virtual stages — model with v chunks per stage: tasks F[i][s][c]. Simpler: implement interleaved as p' = p·v \"virtual stages\" but with the constraint that virtual stages of the same physical device are serialized on one resource and comm goes through physical adjacency... That's more complex. I could implement it: task F[i][s,c] deps: F[i][s,c-1] (same stage same chunk order... within stage, chunk c-1 then c), and F[i][s,c] after F[i][s-1, v-1] (previous stage's last chunk). That's the standard interleaved dependency. Per-device resource serialization: all tasks of device s in a global order determined by the 1F1B-style policy over virtual tasks. That's doable: treat virtual stage vs = s + p*c for the 1F1B ordering, but resource = physical device s. So build a list of \"units\": each unit u in [0, p*v) maps to device dev(u) = u % p (Megatron vLayout interleaved uses virtual stage v of stage s at global position s + v*p), and dependency chain along units 0..pv-1.\n\nThen memory limit per unit: w_limit(u) = pv - 1 - u. And device serialization: only one task per device at a time.\n\nBubble denominator for interleaved: p (physical devices) and per-device total compute = m(f+b) per device.\n\nMakespan ideal = m(f+b)/p_dev... careful: per device total compute = v·m·(f+b)/(v) hmm. With p physical stages, m microbatches, v virtual stages each: per physical device, total compute = m·(f+b) (each microbatch's layer chunk for that device: v chunks each of time (f+b)/v). So define per-chunk times fc = f/v, bc = b/v; device total = m·(f+b). Analytic bubble for interleaved ≈ (p-1)/(v·m + p - 1) — with the caveat of extra comm.\n\nLet me implement and check. Then also derive the general 1F1B formula empirically and fit.\n\nLet's code it.\n\nAlso I should compute the \"drain vs intra-step gap\" decomposition in the simulator to show it's implementable — good for section (e).\n\nLet me write the file into /workspace (artifacts as design attachments? The task workspace is writable). I'll write to /workspace/design/sim_sched.py. That's fine — design artifacts. Let me be careful not to claim it as experimental results on GPUs. It's instrument validation.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nImplementation plan (event-driven):\n\n```python\ndef simulate(p, m, f, b, policy='1f1b', v=1, comm=0.0, units=None):\n    # units: global stage index count = p*v ; device(u) = u % p (interleaved layout) or u (v=1)\n    P = p*v\n    dev = lambda u: u % p if v>1 else u\n    fc, bc = f/v, b/v\n    # tasks: ('F', i, u), ('B', i, u)\n    # per-unit order: forward queue i=0..m-1, backward queue i=0..m-1\n    # deps: F(i,u) needs F(i,u-1) done (u>0) ; F(i,u) needs F(i-1,u)? no; needs device free\n    #       B(i,u) needs F(i,u) done and B(i,u+1) done (u<P-1)\n    # memory limit: unit u can have at most warm(u) outstanding (F done, B not done) beyond? \n```\n\nCareful with the memory limit semantics: In 1F1B, stage u does warmup = P-1-u warmup forwards, so it can hold at most warm(u)+1? Let's define outstanding = number of F completed minus number of B completed for that unit. The constraint: outstanding ≤ warm(u)+? For last unit (u=P-1), warm=0, and it processes F then B immediately, so outstanding max = 1 during that. For unit u, warm(u)=P-1-u, and it holds warm(u) activations waiting + 1 currently being processed. So the rule for issuing a new F: allowed if (#F issued - #B completed) ≤ warm(u). Because before issuing, the number of held activations awaiting B must be < warm(u)... Let's check with the classic: p=4, stage 0 (u=0), warm=3. It issues F0,F1,F2 (3 forwards) then B0, then F3, B1... Standard Megatron: num_warmup = p-1-rank = 3 for rank 0. Yes, it runs 3 forwards before the first backward. So rule: issue F if (nF_issued - nB_done) < warm(u) ... after issuing 3, outstanding = 3 = warm → can't issue more, must do B. Good: condition to issue F: outstanding < warm(u) — but that limits total held to warm. Hmm but the last stage with warm=0 could never issue F by that rule. So condition: outstanding < max(warm(u), ... ) — for warm=0, outstanding must be 0 before issuing F, and it issues F (outstanding becomes 1), then does B. So condition to issue F: outstanding == 0 for warm=0 → general condition: outstanding < warm(u) OR (warm(u)==0 and outstanding==0). Simpler: issue F allowed iff outstanding ≤ warm(u)-1 when warm ≥1, or outstanding==0 when warm==0. Equivalent: outstanding < max(warm,1)? For warm=0 → outstanding < 1 → 0 ✓. For warm=3 → outstanding < max(3,1)=3 ✓. But wait, is holding warm activations + processing correct? Megatron rank 0 with p=4: warmup 3 forwards, then 1F1B steady. During steady: B then F keeps outstanding at 3. Yes.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: outstanding counts activations stored for the unit, and B(i,u) can only complete after B(i,u+1). Fine.\n\nThen scheduling loop: repeat until all tasks done. For each device, consider its candidate next tasks (next F and next B in its queue per policy), pick eligible ones (deps satisfied), choose by policy priority (1F1B: prefer B when possible? Megatron's steady state does F then B alternating; but ordering choice affects makespan). Standard ASAP with priority: prefer B over F when B is ready (drain gradients) — this is roughly what 1F1B does in steady state. Actually 1F1B alternates F,B: it does F first then B in each pair. With ASAP and per-device serialization, whichever is earlier ready gets picked. Ties broken deterministically.\n\nFor a valid 1F1B schedule, the per-device task sequence should be: F^warm, (F,B)^(m-warm), B^warm. I'll enforce that program order rather than greedy: build per-unit task list explicitly, then ASAP-schedule with resource constraints and dependency constraints by iterating to fixpoint. Building explicit per-unit order for 1F1B:\n- F list order: 0..m-1; B list order: 0..m-1\n- interleaved program: first warm F's, then alternate (F,B) for the remaining, then remaining B's. That gives the canonical order.\n\nThen scheduling with dependencies: iterate: for each device, take its next unscheduled task; compute earliest start = max(device_free_time, deps ready times); among all devices, pick the device whose next task has the smallest earliest start (ties deterministic) and commit it. Repeat. This is deterministic list scheduling and gives the canonical schedule.\n\nAFAB: per-unit order = all F then all B, warm limit = m.\n\nInterleaved 1F1B: units P=p*v, warm(u) = P-1-u, device(u)=u%p (Megatron layout: virtual stages are assigned such that device s holds virtual stage with global index s + v'·p). Deps along unit chain u-1 → u.\n\nAlso add optional comm cost: each dependency crossing devices adds comm time (activation/grad send). For interleaved, comm across non-adjacent physical devices (when dev(u-1) != dev(u)-1 mod...) Actually with the interleaved layout, u-1 and u are physically adjacent (dev(u) = dev(u-1)+1 mod p) except at wrap where it's still adjacency? For u = p (which is virtual stage 1 of device 0), u-1 = p-1 = virtual stage 0 of device p-1, physically adjacent ✓. And u=0 has no dep. So comm only at activation crossing.\n\nOK, implement with comm parameter optional.\n\nBubble computation:\n- T = makespan = max completion\n- B_struct = 1 - m(f+b)/(p·T)\n- per-device idle: dev_idle[s] = T - sum(task durations on device s) - comm? (exclude comm)\n- drain decomposition: last compute task end vs... define drain gap = T - (time when device's last backward... hmm). Decomposition: intra-step gap = gaps between consecutive compute tasks on the device; drain = T - last_compute_end_on_device... Actually drain for the last stage = 0 by construction (it finishes last). Drain for stage 0 = T - end of its last backward. Hmm. Better decomposition used in the ZB paper: \"input bubble\" (before first forward), \"warmup bubble\" (idle during warmup of downstream stage), \"output bubble\" (idle while waiting for backward pass completion). Let me use the paper's taxonomy: ZB paper Figure: bubble = input bubble + warmup bubble + output bubble. Where:\n  - input bubble: idle before the first microbatch forward arrives (upstream fill),\n  - warmup bubble: idle caused by the pipeline-fill period where only forwards run (upstream/downstream waiting),\n  - output bubble: idle after the device's last forward until step end while waiting for downstream backward to finish.\nThat's a nice decomposition and it separates the drain. I'll implement: input = start of first compute on device (for stage 0 = 0), warmup = idle accumulated before the device's steady state (during the fill), output = T - end of last compute on device. Simpler and defensible: \n  - pre-idle: t_first_start\n  - mid-idle: T - t_last_end is post-drain? No: post = T - t_last_end (device finished early and waits for step boundary) — this is exactly the \"drain/output\" component.\n  - intra-idle = total idle - pre - post.\nThat's clean: report pre-idle (fill), intra-idle (steady-state gaps), post-idle (drain). Good — satisfies researcher's request to separate drain from intra-step.\n\nLet me code and run for a few configs and check formulas:\n- AFAB/1F1B with f=b: expect bubble = (p-1)/(m+p-1), i.e., T = (m+p-1)(f+b) - ... let's check: with f=b, ideal T_ideal = m(f+b); B = (p-1)/(m+p-1) → T = m(f+b)·(m+p-1)/m = (f+b)(m+p-1). So predicted T = (f+b)(m+p-1). Hmm, is that right for 1F1B? For p=2,m=2,f=b=1: predicted T = 2·(2+2-1)=6. Let's simulate manually: stage1: F0@0-1, F1@1-2, B1? stage0 does F0 (0-1), F1 (1-2) [warm=1 for stage 0 with p=2]. stage1 (warm=0): F0 must wait for F0 of stage0 → 1-2, then B0 needs grad from stage1's B0... wait stage1 IS the last. stage1: F0@1-2, B0@2-3, F1 needs F1 from stage0 (done at 2) → F1@3-4, B1@4-5. Then stage0 B0 needs B0 of stage1 done at 3 → B0@3-4, then B1 needs B1 of stage1 (ends 5) → B1@5-6. T=6 ✓ matches (f+b)(m+p-1)=2·3=6. Great.\n\nFor general f,b, 1F1B: hypothesis T = m(f+b) + (p-1)·max(f,b)? Check p=2,m=2: with f,b general: stage0: F0@0-f, F1@f-2f; stage1: F0@f-2f, B0@2f-2f+b, F1@max(2f, 2f+b)... = 2f+b → ends 2f+b+f; B1 ends 3f+2b; stage0 B0@3f? B0 on stage0 needs B0 stage1 done at 2f+b → B0@max(2f, 2f+b) = 2f+b → ends 2f+2b; B1 needs stage1 B1 done at 3f+2b → B1@3f+2b → ends 3f+3b. T = 3f+3b = (f+b)(m+p-1) regardless of ratio! Hmm interesting. Let me check p=2, m=1: T should be f+b (stage0 F) + ... stage0: F@0-f, stage1 F@f-2f, B@2f-2f+b, stage0 B needs stage1 B done at 2f+b → ends 2f+2b. Formula (f+b)(1+2-1) = 2(f+b) ✓.\n\nSo 1F1B with f,b: T = (f+b)(m+p-1)? That seems to hold when there is no resource conflict... but with b > f, the backward drain dominates and stages may stall differently. Let me test p=4, m=4, f=1, b=2 via simulation. Also test large m: for m=8, p=4, f=1, b=2 → predicted T = 3·(12) = 36? Ideal = m(f+b) = 24 → bubble = 1/3 = (p-1)/(m+p-1) = 3/11 = 0.27 → T = 24/0.727 = 33. So T=33 vs formula (f+b)(m+p-1)=36 → mismatch. So the formula (f+b)(m+p-1) only holds for certain ratio regimes (as expected: for b=2f, steady state is backward-limited and the device alternates differently). Good — this is exactly why the researcher asked for the general formula. So let me derive it via simulation and fit: candidate general 1F1B makespan: T = (p-1)f + m·max(f, b) + ... let's test m=8,p=4,f=1,b=2: (p-1)f + m·max = 3 + 16 = 19, too small. Let's just simulate and look at the numbers.\n\nKnown result from literature (e.g., DeepPipe/Varuna/ \"Optimal pipeline schedules for GEMM\"... or the \"Bubblecache\" paper): with backward = 2 forward, ideal time per microbatch per stage = 3f, and 1F1B makespan T = (m + p - 1)·... hmm. Let me just simulate and fit.\n\nActually the ZB paper's bubble ratio definition: bubble ratio = \"bubble time / (ideal execution time + bubble time)\" where they define ideal = 3·m·n/p (n = layer time, 3n = fwd + bwd + weight-grad, and they count weight gradient as a separate task W!). I should include the weight-gradient task W in the task model (since ZB's whole point is hiding the weight-gradient computation of the first/last microbatches). That's important: ZB-Hitchhiking hides the weight gradient of microbatch 1 into the bubble. So the simulator task model must be F, B (with W separate): F = f, B = b (grad-of-activation only), W = w per microbatch per stage, with W[i][s] after B[i][s], and W on the device's own queue.\n\nSo ideal = m(f+b+w)/p... and in ZB notation, they set f=1,b=2,w=1 (bwd = 2× fwd), ideal step = 3·m·n/p... hmm, actually they say total compute per layer = 3n (f n, bwd 2n including weight grad?). Let me define explicitly in the design: f (forward), b (backward, activation-grad only), w (weight-grad), and declare the convention: the ZB paper uses n for fwd, 2n for bwd (incl. weight grad?) — mark as to-verify (U5). Our simulator takes (f, b, w) explicit and reports T_ideal = m(f+b+w)/p. That removes the ambiguity. Good — that's a design strength: make the convention explicit and configurable.\n\nOK. Now for the empirical fit of 1F1B with (f=1,b=2,w=1): simulate and compare against the standard \"bubble = (p-1)/(m+p-1)\" claim. Let's implement and run.\n\nLet me write the code now. Keep it tight.\n\nThen produce the final design. I'll also estimate GPU-hours for the minimum viable core: configs: p∈{2,4,8} × m∈{p,2p,4p,8p} × conditions. Let me count: core matrix: p 3 × m 4 × conditions (ZB-2p, 1F1B, interleaved v2, AFAB, ZB-β=p maybe) × 2 balance × repeats. Rough GPU-hour estimate: each run: model e.g. GPT-style with hidden size chosen so per-microbatch per-stage fwd+bwd ≈ 100-200 ms on A100/H100 → step ≈ 0.3-0.8 s; 20 iterations + compile/warmup ≈ 60 s per run; plus setup/launch overhead ~3-5 min per run (container, model init, NCCL init). So ~6 min wall × #runs; #runs = 3 p × 4 m × 4 sched × 2 balance = 96 runs; but each run uses p GPUs → GPU-hours = Σ over runs of p × 6min ≈ 96 runs × avg p=4.67 GPUs × 0.1h ≈ 45 GPU-hours → say O(50) GPU-hours, order of magnitude 10^2 GPU-hours, fits in one 8-GPU node in ~6-7 wall-clock hours. Full design (p up to 16, imbalance ±25%, recompute on/off, ratio sweep) → 10^3 GPU-hours. That's a reasonable order-of-magnitude estimate labeled ESTIMATION.\n\nNow write code and run.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "mkdir -p /workspace/design && cat > /workspace/design/sched_sim.py <<'PY'\n\"\"\"CPU-only schedule simulator (design instrument, NOT the GPU experiment).\nTasks per (microbatch i, physical stage s): F (fwd, f), B (act-grad, b), W (weight-grad, w).\nVirtual (interleaved) stages: P = p*v units, unit u -> device u % p, dependency chain along u.\n\"\"\"\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Sim:\n    p: int; m: int; f: float; b: float; w: float; v: int = 1\n    policy: str = \"1f1b\"; limit: int = None\n\n    def run(self):\n        p, m, v = self.p, self.m, self.v\n        P = p * v\n        dev = lambda u: u % p\n        fc, bc, wc = self.f / v, self.b / v, self.w / v\n        warm = P - 1 - self.limit if self.limit is not None else None\n        # build per-device program (list of (kind,i,u)) following canonical order of the policy\n        prog = [[] for _ in range(p)]\n        for u in range(P):\n            s = dev(u); W = warm if warm is not None else max(P - 1 - u, 0)\n            W = min(W, m)\n            if self.policy == \"afab\":\n                seq = [(\"F\", i, u) for i in range(m)] + [(\"B\", i, u) for i in range(m)] + [(\"W\", i, u) for i in range(m)]\n            else:  # 1f1b / interleaved_1f1b: W follows B right away (steady state)\n                warm_seq = [i for i in range(W)]\n                steady = [i for i in range(W, m)]\n                seq = [(\"F\", i, u) for i in warm_seq]\n                for i in steady:\n                    seq += [(\"F\", i, u), (\"B\", i, u), (\"W\", i, u)]\n                for i in reversed(warm_seq):\n                    seq += [(\"B\", i, u), (\"W\", i, u)]\n            prog[s] += seq\n        # deps: F(i,u) after F(i,u-1); B(i,u) after F(i,u) and B(i,u+1); W(i,u) after B(i,u)\n        done, start, end = {}, {}, {}\n        ready = lambda k, i, u: self._dep_ok(k, i, u, done, p, v)\n        ptr = [0]*p; freeat = [0.0]*p\n        deps_done = {}\n        remaining = sum(len(x) for x in prog)\n        while remaining:\n            best = None\n            for s in range(p):\n                if ptr[s] >= len(prog[s]): continue\n                k, i, u = prog[s][ptr[s]]\n                if not self._dep_ready(k, i, u, end, p, v, m): continue\n                st = max(freeat[s], self._dep_time(k, i, u, end, p, v, m))\n                if best is None or (st, s) < best: best = (st, s)\n            if best is None:\n                raise RuntimeError(\"stuck\")\n            st, s = best\n            k, i, u = prog[s][ptr[s]]\n            dur = {\"F\": fc, \"B\": bc, \"W\": wc}[k]\n            e = st + dur\n            start[(k, i, u)] = st; end[(k, i, u)] = e\n            ptr[s] += 1; freeat[s] = e; remaining -= 1\n        T = max(end.values())\n        busy = [0.0]*p\n        last_end = [0.0]*p; first_start = [T]*p\n        tasks_by_dev = [[] for _ in range(p)]\n        for (k, i, u), e in end.items():\n            s = dev(u); d = {\"F\": fc, \"B\": bc, \"W\": wc}[k]\n            busy[s] += d; tasks_by_dev[s].append((start[(k,i,u)], e))\n            last_end[s] = max(last_end[s], e); first_start[s] = min(first_start[s], start[(k,i,u)])\n        rows = []\n        for s in range(p):\n            tl = sorted(tasks_by_dev[s]); gaps = [tl[j+1][0]-tl[j][1] for j in range(len(tl)-1)]\n            intra = sum(g for g in gaps if g > 1e-9)\n            pre = first_start[s]; post = T - last_end[s]\n            rows.append(dict(stage=s, busy=busy[s], idle=T-busy[s], pre=pre, intra=intra, post=post,\n                             bubble=(T-busy[s])/T))\n        agg = 1 - sum(busy)/(p*T)\n        return dict(T=T, agg=agg, per_stage=rows, ideal=m*(self.f+self.b+self.w)/p)\n\n    def _dep_ready(self, k, i, u, end, p, v, m):\n        P = p*v\n        if k == \"F\":\n            if i > 0 and (\"F\", i-1, u) not in end: return False  # same-unit program handled by queue\n            return u == 0 or (\"F\", i, u-1) in end\n        if k == \"B\":\n            return (\"F\", i, u) in end and (u == P-1 or (\"B\", i, u+1) in end)\n        return (\"B\", i, u) in end\n\n    def _dep_time(self, k, i, u, end, p, v, m):\n        P = p*v\n        if k == \"F\": return 0.0 if u == 0 else end[(\"F\", i, u-1)]\n        if k == \"B\":\n            t = end[(\"F\", i, u)]\n            if u < P-1: t = max(t, end[(\"B\", i, u+1)])\n            return t\n        return end[(\"B\", i, u)]\nPY\necho written", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-83694a5d64a7e3d5", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-83694a5d64a7e3d5", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"mkdir -p /workspace/design && cat > /workspace/design/sched_sim.py <<'PY'\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"CPU-only schedule simulator (design instrument, NOT the GPU experiment).\\nTasks per (microbatch i, physical stage s): F (fwd, f), B (act-grad, b), W (weight-grad, w).\\nVirtual (interleaved) stages: P = p*v units, unit u -> device u % p, dependency chain along u.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nfrom dataclasses 