{"id": "286b6785-d533-4905-bb7e-e9647e3e2658", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:08:52.226585", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "ba34409b-e448-4381-ae17-1c472504946e", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ac75a9c642ceceec", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"cd /workspace/design && sed -i 's|if i > 0 and (\\\"F\\\", i-1, u) not in end: return False  # same-unit program handled by queue\\\\n||' sched_sim.py && python3 - <<'PY'\\nimport re\\ns=open('/workspace/design/sched_sim.py').read()\\ns=s.replace('            if i > 0 and (\\\"F\\\", i-1, u) not in end: return False  # same-unit program handled by queue\\\\n','')\\ns=s.replace('for i in reversed(warm_seq):','for i in warm_seq:')\\nopen('/workspace/design/sched_sim.py','w').write(s)\\nPY\\ncd /workspace/design && python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom sched_sim import Sim\\nprint(\\\"p  m  policy      T      agg_bubble   analytic\\\")\\nfor p in (2,4,8):\\n    for m in (p,2*p,4*p,8*p):\\n        for pol in (\\\"afab\\\",\\\"1f1b\\\"):\\n            r=Sim(p=p,m=m,f=1,b=1,w=1,policy=pol).run()\\n            print(f\\\"{p:2d} {m:3d} {pol:11s} {r['T']:6.2f}  {r['agg']:.4f}   {(p-1)/(m+p-1):.4f}\\\")\\nprint(\\\"--- interleaved v=2 (p=4,m=16) vs (p-1)/(v*m+p-1) =\\\", 3/(32+3))\\nr=Sim(p=4,m=16,f=1,b=1,w=1,v=2,policy=\\\"1f1b\\\").run(); print(f\\\"sim agg={r['agg']:.4f} T={r['T']}\\\")\\nPY\", \"exit_code\": 1, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.216, \"output\": \"p  m  policy      T      agg_bubble   analytic\\n 2   2 afab          8.00  0.2500   0.3333\\n 2   2 1f1b         10.00  0.4000   0.3333\\n 2   4 afab         14.00  0.1429   0.2000\\n 2   4 1f1b         20.00  0.4000   0.2000\\n 2   8 afab         26.00  0.0769   0.1111\\n 2   8 1f1b         40.00  0.4000   0.1111\\n 2  16 afab         50.00  0.0400   0.0588\\n 2  16 1f1b         80.00  0.4000   0.0588\\n 4   4 afab         18.00  0.3333   0.4286\\n 4   4 1f1b         30.00  0.6000   0.4286\\n 4   8 afab         30.00  0.2000   0.2727\\n 4   8 1f1b         66.00  0.6364   0.2727\\n 4  16 afab         54.00  0.1111   0.1579\\n 4  16 1f1b        138.00  0.6522   0.1579\\n 4  32 afab        102.00  0.0588   0.0857\\n 4  32 1f1b        282.00  0.6596   0.0857\\n 8   8 afab         38.00  0.3684   0.4667\\n 8   8 1f1b         94.00  0.7447   0.4667\\n 8  16 afab         62.00  0.2258   0.3043\\n 8  16 1f1b        230.00  0.7913   0.3043\\n 8  32 afab        110.00  0.1273   0.1795\\n 8  32 1f1b        502.00  0.8088   0.1795\\n 8  64 afab        206.00  0.0680   0.0986\\n 8  64 1f1b        1046.00  0.8164   0.0986\\n--- interleaved v=2 (p=4,m=16) vs (p-1)/(v*m+p-1) = 0.08571428571428572\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<stdin>\\\", line 9, in <module>\\n  File \\\"/workspace/design/sched_sim.py\\\", line 49, in run\\n    raise RuntimeError(\\\"stuck\\\")\\nRuntimeError: stuck\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "ba34409b-e448-4381-ae17-1c472504946e", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "717d1376-6133-41d8-883f-373803a36ce8", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:11:08.340548", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "286b6785-d533-4905-bb7e-e9647e3e2658", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "El greedy de mi primer simulador introduce artefactos de orden (no es ASAP exacto). Lo reescribo como evaluación exacta de un programa fijo (largo camino en el grafo de eventos), que es lo correcto para el control G1."}], "reasoning_content": "The simulator is buggy: 1F1B is way off, and AFAB is off too. My model has issues:\n\n1. In my model, the backward dependency chain is B(i,u) after B(i,u+1) — that's correct (grad flows downstream to upstream). But I also need F(i,u) to depend on... F(i, u-1). Fine. But the issue: I made the program order per-device, and for 1f1b I emit warmup F's then steady F,B,W then cooldown. My warm computation: `warm = P-1-u` for 1F1B → for p=2, unit 0: warm=1. Cooldown order should be reversed (LIFO in terms of... actually 1F1B cooldown does the remaining backwards in the same order as warmup forwards, i.e., increasing i). Hmm I changed it to increasing which is correct for 1F1B (FIFO order of remaining microbatches).\n\nBut the big problem: the greedy \"pick device with smallest earliest start\" with strict per-device program order... The result T=80 for p=2,m=16 1f1b vs analytic expected: with unit time (f+b+w)=3 per microbatch per stage, ideal per-device busy = 3m = 48, both devices busy = 96; bubble (p-1)/(m+p-1)=5.88% → T = 96/(2*(1-0.0588)) ... T = busy_total/(p(1-B)) = 96/(2*0.9412) = 51. But sim gives 80 → 0.4 bubble. So my dependency model is too restrictive/wrong.\n\nKey bug: I required B(i,u) to depend on B(i,u+1) AND F(i,u) on F(i,u-1) — correct. But also, in AFAB, T should be m*unit + (p-1)*unit for the drain. For p=2,m=2 afab: expected T = (m+p-1)*unit = 3*3=9, sim gives 8. Hmm, sim gives 8 < 9, meaning sim is infeasible/too optimistic for AFAB. And 1f1b gives 10 > 9.\n\nSo the AFAB result is *too good* → my deps are insufficient. What's missing? In AFAB with W: for device 0, program = F F B B W W. The constraint that gradient for microbatch i at device 0 requires B(i, device1) done — I have that. And W after B(i,u). Hmm, why is 8 feasible? Let's trace p=2, m=2, unit times f=b=w=1.\n\nDevice0 (u=0): prog F0,F1,B0,B1,W0,W1. Device1 (u=1): prog F0,F1,B0,B1,W0,W1.\nt=0: dev0 F0 (ready, u=0) st=0 → end 1. dev1 F0 needs F(0,0) not done. So greedy picks dev0 F0 → [0,1].\nNext: dev0 F1 st=1 end 2; dev1 F0 ready at 1. Both at st=1, tie → pick smaller stage = dev0 F1 → [1,2]. Hmm that's the greedy flaw: it should pick... whatever, both st=1. Then dev1 F0 at 2 → [2,3]. dev0 B0 needs B(0,1) not done; dev0 waits. dev1 B0 needs F(0,1) done(3) and u=P-1 → ready at 3 → [3,4]. dev1 F1: program order says after F0 → st=max(4, end F(1,0)=2)=4 → [4,5]. dev0 B0 needs B(0,1) done at 4 → st 4 → [4,5]. dev1 B1: after F1(5) and ... =5 → [5,6]. dev0 W0 after B(0,0)=5 → [5,6]. dev0 B1 needs B(1,1) done 6 → [6,7]. dev1 W0 [6,7], W1 [7,8]. dev0 W1 after B1=7 → [7,8]. T=8.\n\nIs this schedule feasible? Check dev1 program: F0[2,3], F1[4,5] — but F1 for dev1 should be able to run at [3,4] (after F0 ends at 3, and F(1,0) done at 2). Greedy gave it 4 because dev1's program pointer was at B0 which became ready first. That's just a suboptimal ordering — but it should be AFAB order F,F,B,B,W,W so B0 shouldn't block F1... The pointer model means dev1's next task is F1 right after F0 (program F0,F1,B0,...). Wait no: for p=2, afab device1 prog = F0,F1,B0,B1,W0,W1. So after F0 at [2,3], pointer is at F1, ready at max(3, end F(1,0)=2) = 3 → [3,4]. But in my trace the greedy picked dev0's W... no wait. Let me recheck: after F0[2,3] on dev1, candidates: dev0 B0 (needs B(0,1) - not done → skip), dev1 F1 ready at 3 → [3,4]. Then dev0 B0 not ready, dev1 B0 ready at 4 → [4,5]. Then dev0 B0 ready at 5 → [5,6]; dev1 B1 needs F(1,1) done at 4 → but program pointer dev1 at B1 after B0: ready max(5, 4)=5 → [5,6]. Then dev0 W0 [6,7], dev1 W0... etc. T would be ~9-10. My earlier trace was wrong because greedy tie-break chose dev0 F1 before dev1 F0, delaying dev1 by one. That's a *greedy ordering artifact* — makespan should ideally use ASAP with earliest-start priority over all ready tasks, and I do pick min earliest start with tie by stage. At the t=1 step, dev0 F1 (st=1) and dev1 F0 (st=1) tie; picking dev1 F0 first is better. The tie-break should prefer... it's a list-scheduling heuristic. For exact analytic validation I should instead compute ASAP schedule where each task starts at max(dep time, device free) resolved by iterative relaxation (fixed point) rather than greedy commit. \n\nBetter approach: model as a simple longest-path / list scheduling over tasks with resources via iterative computation:\n- For each device, tasks in program order: t_0 < t_1 < ...\n- Each task earliest start = max(device_free, dep times). Compute by iterating until fixpoint: initialize all starts at 0, repeatedly compute start = max(prev_task_end_on_device, dep times) in program order until no changes. That's a standard longest-path in an event graph and converges to the ASAP (optimal given fixed program orders). That's the right semantics and it's deterministic.\n\nAlso need to double check analytic formulas. With unit u = f+b+w = 3 per microbatch per stage:\n- AFAB bubble = (p-1)/(m+p-1). For p=2,m=2: (1)/(3)=0.333 → T = total_busy/(p(1-B)) = (2*2*3)/(2*0.667)=6/0.667=9 → T=9. Good, expected T=9.\n- 1F1B same bubble ratio (p-1)/(m+p-1) under f=b and W immediately following B. Good.\n- interleaved v: (p-1)/(v*m + p - 1).\n\nLet me rewrite with a fixpoint ASAP and proper program orders. For interleaved, the program order per physical device must interleave virtual stages properly. Simpler approach for the design-validation purpose: treat \"interleaved\" as PP with P=p*v units but with the resource constraint that units sharing a device are serialized. Use unit-indexed program order: for device s, program = for each microbatch phase in 1f1b order over units u ≡ s mod p... This is getting complicated. The cleanest: build the event graph with tasks F(i,u),B(i,u),W(i,u), deps as above, plus per-device program order derived from 1F1B over units with virtual-stage warmup counts.\n\nMegatron interleaved 1F1B: total virtual model chunks v; for virtual stage index u (0..P-1), warmup = min(m, P-1-u)... roughly. And within a physical device, the order of execution follows the order in which virtual stages are processed in the 1F1B loop: for each iteration k, device s processes its virtual stages u = s, s+p, s+2p, ... in increasing order for forward. I'll approximate that: program per device = merge of unit programs by microbatch step index.\n\nI think for the purpose of this design deliverable, I need: (1) a working ASAP event-graph simulator; (2) verification of AFAB and 1F1B analytic formulas (G1); (3) interleaved v=2 formula check; (4) demonstrate decomposition into pre/intra/post (drain) gaps; (5) show a ZB-Hitchhiking-style schedule (β=2p, hide backward of first microbatch) achieving lower/zero structural bubble with more memory — as instrument demonstration only, flagged as to-verify.\n\nGiven time/budget, let me implement a cleaner generic simulator with explicit \"schedule generator\" producing a program (list of tasks) per device, then ASAP fixpoint evaluation, and verify. For ZB, I'll implement ZB-Hitchhiking-ish via a limit parameter (max pending backward) — actually let me keep it simpler: implement a generic \"policy\" where warmup count per stage is a free parameter, plus a \"hide first microbatch backward in gaps\" mode. Actually the cleanest demonstration of a zero-bubble-ish schedule in my model: ZB-Hitchhiking is equivalent to: schedule = AFAB-like forward ordering, but each stage performs the backward of microbatch 1 whenever idle. I can implement as program: F for all microbatches (with the constraint of holding activations), and B(i,u) inserted into idle slots ASAP. That's what a \"greedy idle-filler\" policy does. Implementing that as a policy in a fixpoint ASAP model is hard because idle-filling depends on computed timing.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: implement a proper discrete-event greedy scheduler with priorities (event-driven, commit when a task's earliest start is minimal across all ready tasks — an ASAP list schedule). The bug above was the tie-break; using \"pick the task with smallest earliest start, tie-break by task type preference (B before F when both ready? or by dependency depth/criticality)\". Using critical-path priority (longest remaining path) would give near-optimal ASAP and reproduce analytic formulas for AFAB/1F1B.\n\nHmm. This is a rabbit hole; the deliverable is a *design*, not a working simulator. But the researcher asked to include the simulator as a design artifact with unit tests, and validate G1 formulas. I've already learned something valuable: getting an *exact* ASAP simulator right is non-trivial (greedy tie-breaks change makespan), which is itself a design risk worth reporting. Let me spend a couple more steps to get a correct simulator with the criticality-priority ASAP list scheduler and check the AFAB/1F1B/interleaved formulas. That's genuinely useful for the design's G1 gate and cost estimate.\n\nLet me rewrite cleanly.\n\nPriority: use \"longest path to end\" (critical path length) as static priority. Longest path: for a task, its successors are program-order next on device (dynamic) — can't know statically. So use static dependency-graph critical path ignoring device contention: for F(i,u): successors F(i,u+1) chain; for B(i,u): successor B(i,u-1). Compute longest path in dep DAG ignoring resources: cp(F(i,u)) = dur + max over successors. Then priority = earliest start + cp (i.e., LPT/\"highest level\" first). Standard list scheduling with \"level\" priority is a well-known good heuristic.\n\nBut even optimal ASAP with fixed program order = fixpoint longest path — that's the exact evaluation of a given program. The issue is choosing the program. For AFAB and 1F1B, the program order is fully specified (all F's then all B's; and warmup/steady/cooldown). So exact evaluation via fixpoint is right. For a fixed program, resource contention is captured by the \"program-order edge\" with weight = duration → longest path gives exact ASAP makespan. My greedy was wrong; fixpoint is exact. Let me implement fixpoint.\n\nProgram order for AFAB device s: F(0..m-1, u=s), B(0..m-1,u=s), W(0..m-1,u=s). Exact.\nProgram order for 1F1B device s: warmup F(i) for i<Warm, then for i in [Warm..m): F,B,W; then cooldown B,W for i<Warm. Wait, standard 1F1B: warmup does only forwards (no W). Then steady: F,B,W. Then cooldown: B,W for remaining. Yes.\n\nFixpoint: iterate over all tasks computing start = max(program_prev_end, dep_times) until stable. Since it's a DAG (program edges within device + dep edges), a topological fixpoint converges. Just iterate a few passes until no change (Bellman-Ford style).\n\nLet me redo with that. Also need deps: F(i,u) after F(i,u-1); B(i,u) after F(i,u) and B(i,u+1); W(i,u) after B(i,u). Plus for 1F1B memory limit implicit in program.\n\nFor AFAB, T should be 9 for p=2,m=2 with unit 3. Let's check by hand via longest path: AFAB p=2,m=2: chain: F(0,0)[0-3] wait unit time f+b+w=3 but tasks individually 1 each. Let's trust the sim.\n\nLet me implement.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat > /workspace/design/sched_sim.py <<'PY'\n\"\"\"CPU-only schedule simulator -- DESIGN INSTRUMENT ONLY (no GPU, no experiment).\n\nModel: p physical stages (devices), m microbatches, v virtual stages per device.\nTasks per (microbatch i, unit u): F (forward, f), B (activation-grad, b), W (weight-grad, w).\nUnits: u in [0, p*v); device(u) = u % p; pipeline chain runs along u.\nProgram order per device is FIXED by the policy; makespan/idle are then computed EXACTLY\nas a longest-path (ASAP) fixpoint over {program edges, dependency edges}.\n  deps: F(i,u) <- F(i,u-1);  B(i,u) <- F(i,u), B(i,u+1);  W(i,u) <- B(i,u)\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\ndef program(p, m, v, policy, warmup=None):\n    P = p * v\n    dev = lambda u: u % p\n    prog = [[] for _ in range(p)]\n    for u in range(P):\n        s = dev(u)\n        if policy == \"afab\":\n            W = m\n        elif policy == \"1f1b\":                       # canonical 1F1B / interleaved-1F1B\n            W = (P - 1 - u) if warmup is None else warmup(u)\n            W = max(0, min(W, m))\n        elif policy == \"zb_hh\":                      # ZB-Hitchhiking-like: warmup=p-1, B of mb0 hidden in idle\n            W = (P - 1 - u) if warmup is None else warmup(u)\n            W = max(0, min(W, m))\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(policy)\n        steady = list(range(W, m)); cool = list(range(W))\n        seq = [(\"F\", i, u) for i in range(W)]\n        for i in steady:\n            seq += [(\"F\", i, u), (\"B\", i, u)] + ([(\"W\", i, u)] if policy != \"zb_hh\" else [])\n        for i in cool:\n            seq += [(\"B\", i, u), (\"W\", i, u)]\n        if policy == \"zb_hh\":                        # weight-grads pushed to the end (hitchhiked into idle)\n            seq += [(\"W\", i, u) for i in range(m)]\n        prog[s] += seq\n    return prog\n\ndef simulate(p, m, f, b, w, v=1, policy=\"1f1b\", warmup=None):\n    P = p * v\n    dev = lambda u: u % p\n    fc, bc, wc = f / v, b / v, w / v\n    dur = {\"F\": fc, \"B\": bc, \"W\": wc}\n    prog = program(p, m, v, policy, warmup)\n    task_of = {}\n    for s in range(p):\n        for k, i, u in prog[s]:\n            task_of[(k, i, u)] = (s, dur[k])\n    # program edges\n    edges_from = {}            # pred -> list of succ\n    start = {t: 0.0 for t in task_of}\n    for s in range(p):\n        for a, bl in zip(prog[s], prog[s][1:]):\n            edges_from.setdefault(a, []).append(bl)\n    def dep_preds(t):\n        k, i, u = t\n        out = []\n        if k == \"F\":\n            if u > 0: out.append((\"F\", i, u - 1))\n        elif k == \"B\":\n            out.append((\"F\", i, u))\n            if u < P - 1: out.append((\"B\", i, u + 1))\n        else:\n            out.append((\"B\", i, u))\n        return out\n    for t in task_of:\n        for pr in dep_preds(t):\n            edges_from.setdefault(pr, []).append(t)\n    # Bellman-Ford style longest path (DAG; small graphs -> iterate to fixpoint)\n    end = lambda t: start[t] + task_of[t][1]\n    for _ in range(4 * len(task_of) + 10):\n        changed = False\n        for t in task_of:\n            st = start[t]\n            for nx in edges_from.get(t, ()):\n                if end(t) > start(nx):\n                    start[nx] = end(t); changed = True\n        if not changed: break\n    T = max(end(t) for t in task_of)\n    per = {}\n    for s in range(p): per[s] = []\n    for t, (s, d) in task_of.items(): per[s].append((start[t], end(t), t))\n    rows = []\n    for s in range(p):\n        tl = sorted(per[s]); busy = sum(d for _, _, d in [(a, c, c-a) for a, c, _ in tl])\n        busy = sum(c - a for a, c, _ in tl)\n        intra = sum(tl[j+1][0] - tl[j][1] for j in range(len(tl) - 1))\n        pre, post = tl[0][0], T - tl[-1][1]\n        rows.append(dict(stage=s, busy=busy, idle=T - busy, bubble=(T - busy) / T,\n                         pre=pre, intra=intra, post=post))\n    tot_busy = sum(r[\"busy\"] for r in rows)\n    return dict(T=T, agg=1 - tot_busy / (p * T), per_stage=rows,\n                ideal=m * (f + b + w) / p,\n                drain=max(r[\"post\"] for r in rows), intra=max(r[\"intra\"] for r in rows))\nPY\ncd /workspace/design && python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom sched_sim import simulate\nprint(\"G1 check: aggregate bubble vs analytic (p-1)/(m+p-1)   [f=b=w=1]\")\nok=True\nfor p in (2,4,8):\n    for m in (p,2*p,4*p,8*p):\n        for pol in (\"afab\",\"1f1b\"):\n            r=simulate(p,m,1,1,1,policy=pol); a=(p-1)/(m+p-1)\n   