{"id": "9e9cdc0e-0192-48cb-a7d5-9d7a6a4b4898", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:17:42.176545", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d5873fb4-28b9-4414-a441-610b1d18f280", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "El generador de programa 1F1B tenía mal el orden warmup/steady (invertía backward de warmup al final). Reescribo limpio y revalido."}], "reasoning_content": "AFAB matches exactly, 1F1B doesn't. Because in my program for 1F1B, I put W immediately after B in the steady state — wait, but with w=0, W tasks have duration 0 and shouldn't matter. Hmm, the bubble 0.87 for p=8 — that's suspicious: my B ordering. Let me think: my 1F1B program per unit u uses W = p-1-s warmup, but for the *interleaved/1f1b* I generate seq per device combining units. For v=1, units=[s], W = min(m, p-1-s). Then steady F(i),B(i),W(i) for i from W to m; cooldown B(i),W(i) for i in range(W) — cooldown in increasing i.\n\nProblem: In 1F1B, backward order must be in increasing i (FIFO) — yes that's what I do. Hmm.\n\nWait, why bubble 0.87 for p=8, m=128? With w=0 the total busy per device = m*(f+b)=256, and T should be ~ (m+p-1)*(f+b) = 135*2=270 → bubble = 1-256/270 = 0.0519. Getting 0.87 means T ~ 1975, roughly 7x larger. Looks like dependency edges create artificial serialization: my B dependency B(i,u) <- B(i,u+1) requires the same microbatch's downstream backward, correct. And program edge. Hmm, but the issue is my dependency edge for F: F(i,u) <- F(i,u-1). Correct.\n\nAh — I see the bug: in `_prog_1f1b`, for policy \"1f1b\" the W variable computed at the top of the function (the weird ternary line) is unused... but then, cooldown loop: `for i in range(W): seq += [B(i), W(i)]`. Fine.\n\nHmm what about the \"F(i,u) <- F(i,u-1)\" edge combined with program order: For stage 0 with W = p-1 = 7 warmup forwards... that's fine.\n\nWait — maybe the bug: the dependency `B(i,u) <- B(i,u+1)` requires B(i,u+1) to exist as a task. With v=1 that's u+1 = next stage; exists. OK.\n\nLet me just debug quickly: for p=2, m=2, v=1, w=0: expected T = (m+p-1)*(f+b) = 3*2=6, bubble 1 - 4/6 = 0.333. Got 0.4286 → T = 4/(1-0.4286)=7.\n\nLet me trace: stage0 units=[0], W=min(2, 1)=1: seq = [F0] then steady i=1: F1,B1,W1(dur 0) then cooldown i=0: B0,W0.\nSo stage0 program: F0, F1, B1, W1, B0, W0. ← BUG! In 1F1B, the cooldown should run the warmup microbatches' backward, but the *steady state* should start at i=W and the backward of microbatch W happens after... Actually canonical 1F1B stage s: warmup F_0..F_{W-1}; then for i=W..m-1: B_{i-W}, F_i (or F then B); then cooldown B_{m-W}..B_{m-1}. My ordering does F_i then B_i for i>=W and then B_0..B_{W-1} at the very end. That's wrong: the warmup microbatches' backward should happen first (they're oldest), immediately after warmup, in the steady state slots. My \"cooldown at end\" ordering forces B0 (which depends on B0 of stage1) late, causing serialization.\n\nCorrect 1F1B program per unit:\nwarmup: F_0..F_{W-1}\nsteady for j in 0..(m-W-1): B_j, F_{W+j}   (or F then B — Megatron does forward then backward within the same slot: \"1F1B\" alternates; using F then B is fine but then backward index is j and forward W+j... Megatron: warmup forwards 0..W-1; then loop: forward(i), backward(i-W) for i=W..m-1; then backward(m-W..m-1).)\nSo steady: for i in W..m-1: F_i, B_{i-W}; cooldown: B_{m-W}..B_{m-1}.\n\nThat's the correct canonical order. My generator had it inverted. Let me fix: steady emits F_i then B_{i-W} (and W_{i-W} for weight grad with duration w). Also for interleaved, Megatron's interleaved schedule uses similar offsets per model chunk.\n\nAlso the AFAB case matched, so the framework is right. Let me fix the 1F1B program.\n\nAlso, need to fix the interleaved program: for interleaved-1F1B in Megatron with v chunks per device and P=p*v units, the warmup for the first chunk of device s is num_warmup = min(m, (p-1-s) + (v-1)*p)... Megatron: total_virtual_stages = p*v; for virtual stage index v_idx on rank s: num_warmup_microbatches = min(m, total_virtual_stages - (rank + v_idx*world_size) - 1)... Actually in Megatron's forward_backward_pipelining_with_interleaving: \nnum_warmup_microbatches = min(total_num_microbatches, total_virtual_stages - ((pipeline_rank * config.virtual_num_model_chunks) + (model_chunk_id * pipeline_size)) - 1) / pipeline_size ... hmm there's a division. Roughly = ceil((total_virtual_stages - virtual_stage_index - 1)/pipeline_size).\n\nGiven time, I'll implement: warm(u) for unit u = max(0, ceil((P-1-u)/p)) as the per-unit warmup, and per-device program built as: iterate over \"global slots\" — simpler approach: build program per device as:\n warmup phase: for k in 0..W_dev-1: for each chunk c: F(k? ...) \n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me use a cleaner formulation: for each *unit* u (virtual stage), define warmup count w_u = min(m, max(0, ceil((P-1-u)/p))). The per-device program is then the merge over chunks: \n- warmup forwards per chunk c: F(i,u) for i in 0..w_u-1\n- steady: for the device, iterate a global step counter; at each step, for each chunk c forward the next pending i, then for each chunk in reverse, backward the oldest pending warmup microbatch.\n- cooldown: remaining backwards per chunk.\n\nTotal warmup slots for a device W_dev = max over chunks of w_u.\n\nProgram: \nseq = []\nfor k in 0..W_dev-1: for c in chunks: if k < w_{u(c)}: emit F(k, u)\nthen steady: for k in W_dev..m-1: for c in chunks: emit F(k,u) ; for c in reversed chunks: if pending backward exists for that unit: emit B(oldest pending, u) + W\nthen cooldown: while any pending: for c in reversed: emit next pending B + W.\n\nThat's a reasonable canonical interleaved-1F1B. With v=1, w_u = p-1-u = p-1-s and the program reduces to standard 1F1B.\n\nI'll implement this generically and validate AFAB, 1F1B (v=1) exact vs textbook, and interleaved v=2 vs (p-1)/(vm+p-1) (may be approximate).\n\nI'm spending a lot of steps; budget 60, used ~13. That's fine. But I should be careful: this