{"id": "e0166b1b-1101-4e33-8561-ea0b2e7e170a", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:19:12.297014", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "e44ab21a-db11-4302-ac8e-3bc359049256", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "G1 for AFAB and 1F1B (v=1) now matches textbook exactly over 40 configs — excellent instrument validation.\n\nThe interleaved case has a cycle: because for v>1, my per-unit sequence order plus dependency edges create a cycle? Device order edges: merged round-robin across units; program edge from a task in unit u (chunk 0) to a task in unit u+p... and dependency edges F(i,u+1) <- F(i,u). If the program order within a device puts a later unit's task before an earlier unit's task, and the dependency requires the reverse... Let me think: cycle arises when program order on device says A before B, and dependency path says B before A. For interleaved, forward order on device must follow chunk order (u then u+p), which matches dependency F(i,u+1)<-F(i,u) only when consecutive chunk boundaries align. My merged sequence at index k takes seqs[0][k], seqs[1][k] — that's chunk0 then chunk1 (increasing u) which is consistent for F. But for B, dependencies go the other way: B(i,u) needs B(i,u+1) done. My sequence for chunk1's B comes at same index k as chunk0's B — program order puts chunk0's B(i, u) BEFORE chunk1's B(i, u+p)... that's a conflict → cycle when combined with dependency B(i,u) <- B(i,u+p)? Wait dependency: B(i,u) waits for B(i, u+1), i.e., edge from B(i,u+1) → B(i,u). On the same device, units u and u+p are consecutive (chunk c and c+1). Program order puts B of chunk c before B of chunk c+1 (same k index), but dependency requires B(i,u+p) → B(i,u), i.e., chunk c+1 first. Conflict = cycle. In real Megatron interleaved, backward chunk order is descending. So I should merge with descending unit order for B tasks. Fix: when merging at index k, emit F-tasks in ascending chunk order and B-tasks in descending chunk order. Simplest: build merged by iterating k, collecting one task per sequence, then stably sorting: F's ascending u first, then B's descending u. Let me implement that.\n\nActually the real Megatron interleaved order: forward pass iterates over model chunks 0..v-1 for a given microbatch, backward iterates chunks v-1..0. But per device (stage), each device handles the same chunk sequence. Since program edges are just a heuristic ordering (they encode device serialization order), the exact order affects the makespan; for the G1 control of interleaved I want the canonical Megatron order.\n\nI'm spending a lot of steps on this. The key deliverable is the design, and I've already validated G1 exactly for AFAB/1F1B (40 configs) and demonstrated the pre/intra/drain decomposition. The interleaved control is secondary. But it's referenced as a baseline, so having the instrument produce its structural bubble is useful. Let me do one focused fix (B descending chunk order in merge), then move on regardless of the outcome.\n\nNote also the timing: this all runs fast (<1s).\n\nLet me apply the fix.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cd /workspace/design && python3 - <<'PY'\ns=open('sched_sim.py').read()\nold = \"\"\"        merged = []\n        for k in range(max(len(x) for x in seqs)):\n            for sq in seqs:\n                if k < len(sq): merged.append(sq[k])\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"        merged = []\n        for k in range(max(len(x) for x in seqs)):\n            row = [sq[k] for sq in seqs if k < len(sq)]\n            # canonical interleaved order: forwards ascend chunk index, backwards descend\n            row.sort(key=lambda t: (0 if t[0] == \"F\" else 1, t[2] if t[0] == \"F\" else -t[2]))\n            merged += row\"\"\"\nassert old in s\nopen('sched_sim.py','w').write(s.replace(old,new)); print(\"patched\")\nPY\ncd /workspace/design && timeout 300 python3 - <<'PY'\nimport time\nfrom sched_sim import simulate, peak_activation_slots\nbad=[]\nfor p in (2,4,8,16):\n    for m in (p,2*p,4*p,8*p,16*p):\n        ana=(p-1)/(m+p-1)\n        for pol in (\"afab\",\"1f1b\"):\n            r=simulate(p,m,1,1,0,policy=pol)\n            if abs(r['agg']-ana)>1e-12: bad.append((p,m,pol,round(r['agg'],6),round(ana,6)))\nprint(\"G1 v=1 AFAB/1F1B vs (p-1)/(m+p-1):\", bad or \"EXACT over 40 configs\")\nfor (p,v,m) in ((4,2,16),(8,2,32),(8,4,64),(16,4,64)):\n    try:\n        r=simulate(p,m,1,1,0,v=v,policy=\"1f1b\"); a=(p-1)/(v*m+p-1)\n        print(f\"  interleaved p={p} v={v} m={m}: sim={r['agg']:.6f} ana={a:.6f} {'OK' if abs(r['agg']-a)<1e-12 else 'DELTA %.2e'%abs(r['agg']-a)}\")\n    except AssertionError as e: print(f\"  interleaved p={p} v={v} m={m}: {e}\")\nfor (f,b) in ((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)):\n    r=simulate(8,64,f,b,0.5); print(f\"  ratio f={f} b={b} w=0.5 -> T={r['T']:7.2f} agg={r['agg']:.4f}\")\nr0=simulate(8,64,1,2,1); r1=simulate(8,64,1,2,1,imbalance=lambda u:1.25 if u==4 else 1.0)\nprint(f\"  imbalance: balanced agg={r0['agg']:.4f} T={r0['T']:.1f} | +25% mid agg={r1['agg']:.4f} T={r1['T']:.1f}\")\nprint(\"  drain vs intra (p=8 m=64 f=1 b=2 w=1):\")\nfor row in r0['per_stage']: print(f\"    s{row['stage']} idle={row['idle']:7.2f} pre={row['pre']:6.2f} intra={row['intra']:7.2f} drain={row['post']:6.2f}\")\nt=time.time()\nfor pol in (\"1f1b\",\"afab\",\"zb2p\"):\n    r=simulate(8,64,1,2,1,policy=pol)\n    print(f\"  demo {pol:5s} T={r['T']:8.2f} agg={r['agg']:.4f} peak_act_slots={peak_activation_slots(8,64,policy=pol)}\")\nprint(f\"  wall {time.time()-t:.2f}s CPU;  p=16,m=128 timing:\", end=\" \")\nt=time.time(); simulate(16,128,1,2,1); print(f\"{time.time()-t:.2f}s\")\nPY", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ae88db2dd626d8cb", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-ae88db2dd626d8cb", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /workspace/design && python3 - <<'PY'\\ns=open('sched_sim.py').read()\\nold = \\\"\\\"\\\"        merged = []\\n    